  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 239 AM CDT Tue Sep 15 2026\n\nSatellite and webcam observations indicate a couple of patches of \nlingering fog this morning over eastern South Dakota (and along I-29 \nin Deuel County in particular) as of roughly 07Z. As winds increase \nover that area, the expectation is that the remaining fog will clear \nout over the next couple of hours. Gusty winds have arrived over \nnorth central South Dakota this morning, up to 45 to 50 miles per \nhour at times. Given the delayed onset of the winds and the \npersistence of 40+ mile per hour gusts, have extended the Wind \nAdvisory through 12Z this morning for all counties previously under \nthe Advisory. No additional counties have been added at this time. \nWinds are expected to die down through the day today as a high \npressure center builds in. With this high pressure overhead, light \nwinds, and nearly full humidity recovery overnight, another round of \nfog will be possible early Wednesday morning, mainly over northeast \nSouth Dakota and western Minnesota. \n\nOver the next few days, a negatively tilted ridge will build over \nthe Northern Plains, while and upper-level low develops over the \nwestern CONUS. This setup will allow for a warmer airmass to build \ninto the region, increasing high temperatures by the end of the \nweek. Latest NBM median values sit in the mid to upper 70s over \nnearly the entire forecast area Thursday and beyond, which is near-\nnormal to slightly above normal. \n\nThe upper-level setup will allow for stronger winds aloft to develop \na weak low pressure center with some rain out ahead. No severe \nweather is expected with these rain showers, and neither is much \nsignificant precipitation. Latest NBM probability of seeing a tenth \nof an inch through 06Z Thursday is generally less than 20 percent \nacross the forecast area, with some pockets of 20 to 30 percent over \ncentral South Dakota. \n\nMore robust chances for rain come into the forecast area Friday into \nSaturday as the upper-level low mentioned in the above paragraph \nmoves eastward into the Northern Plains. There is some disagreement \non the timing within model guidance, but there seems to be strong \nagreement amongst the ensembles (NBM, GFS, Euro, and Canadian \nspecifically) in the track of the highest precipitation totals. At \nthe moment, it appears that the axis of heaviest precipitation will \nremain to the south, running through or just north of Sioux Falls. \nThis means that eastern South Dakota will be the area within the \nAberdeen CWA that is set to receive the most rainfall, with totals \ndecreasing moving to the northwest. Still a fairly large degree of \nspread exists over eastern South Dakota, with the NBM 25th/75th \npercentile rainfall totals ending Saturday morning ranges from 1\"-3\" \nin total. Euro and Canadian ensembles are generally in agreement \nwith that spread, although are perhaps a bit lower on the 75th \npercentiles. The GEFS is a bit of an outlier in that it is much \nlower, having a spread around 0.5\"-1.5\". Given how it verified with \nrecent rainfall, confidence would lean against trusting those lower \nvalues. All this to say that these QPF totals raise somewhat of a \nred flag with regards to the flooding potential. Will dive more in-\ndepth on the flooding risk in future discussions, but to give a \nbrief overview: Storm motion, warm cloud layer, and high PWATs all \ngive additional credence to the flooding threat over eastern South \nDakota during this event. Through 12Z Friday, a Slight Risk \nExcessive Rainfall Outlook is in place for portions of eastern and \ncentral South Dakota, with a Marginal through 12Z Saturday.",
